								<div class="title" id="post-title-2475068">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610139848" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1458316644 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2016 15:57</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2475068">
									

<p>There is the core of an interesting idea here, but I'm not sold on the presentation at present. It could have done with a lot more polishing before being posted. Leaving aside the final note (which, now that the Keter request is obfuscated, doesn't seem to have a reason to exist) the Series I style is not particularly logically cogent, and leaves me with a number of questions:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Expand&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Collapse&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>recontainment becomes unfeasible</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While &quot;unfeasible&quot; is technically correct, I would suggest that &quot;infeasible&quot; is more common.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>vaguely humanoid entity</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The imprecision of &quot;vaguely humanoid&quot; (in what way is it not humanoid?) conflicts with clinical tone.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>primarily constructed from 316 surgical steel and consumer-grade electronics</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can't parse this phrase. If &quot;surgical steel and consumer-grade&quot; is an adjectival phrase, then I don't think that many consumer grade electronics (or any electronics, really) are made of surgical steel. Also, I don't understand how to picture &quot;316 &#8230; electronics&quot; - should that be &quot;316 items of consumer electronic goods&quot;?</p>
<p>Otherwise, it feels like a word is missing after &quot;surgical steel&quot;, and that there is a mix of items, some of which are surgical steel [somethings], and the rest are consumer-grade electronics.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Entity stands at</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this needs an article: &quot;The entity stands&#8230;&quot;. The same is true for &quot;Forelimbs possess&#8230;&quot; and &quot;Exterior covering&#8230;&quot; later in the paragraph - these sound ilke observational notes, rather than a later-written description.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a hunched posture. It resembles a distorted human in shape &#8230;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, my view is that &quot;hunched&quot; and &quot;distorted human&quot; are not particularly precise for clinical tone - the picture I have is of a gorilla-like posture, so it may be better to use the phrase &quot;gorilline posture and gait&quot; and do away with &quot;knucklewalking&quot; as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Exterior covering originally consisted of lacquered sheet steel but is presently 80% damaged or missing</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does the Foundation know what the exterior cover consisted of, or even that there was one?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2878 exhibits human or above-human intelligence but distinctly non-human psychology</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I find it difficult to imagine that the Foundation can ascribe psychology to something that they've only just started to communicate with. I think this would be better describing behaviour, and letting readers judge psychology for themselves.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It possesses great proficiency in the areas of generalized logical reasoning and problem-solving</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does the Foundation know this? What logic problems have they set for this skip to solve?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It appears to lack any form of social intelligence or awareness and</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does Addendum 1 display social intelligence of the skip?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2878 is predatory and extremely hostile to humans, attacking on sight</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Attacking on sight is not predatory behaviour. Predators hunt, which is quite different. How does the Foundation know that this skip is predatory?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Victims are pinned down and vivisected, having a number of their organs removed and prepared for transplantation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Addendum 1 says the relevant incident was the first time that the skip had attempted to integrate new parts. As a result, I don't think the Foundation can be so certain that this is what the skip always does to its victims.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All senses appear to function normally</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How can the Foundation know that taste and smell are functioning normally? Can taste function normally - I presume the tongue is missing along with the lower jaw.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>vocal chords and central nervous system</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Vocal &quot;cords&quot; is correct - although &quot;vocal folds&quot; is even more accurate.</p>
<p>And if this thing has vocal cords, but no mouth, tongue or lips, how could it possibly create sounds that are intelligible as words. Try it - hold your mouth open, don't move your lips or tongue at all, and say &quot;help&quot;. Or &quot;amigo&quot;. Or a series of numbers in Spanish. There's no way that the Foundation would have understood what the skip is saying.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hello, SCP-2878</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How is this thing expected to know it's name?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[SCP-2878 does not display any physical signs of having noticed the drone.]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does the Foundation know that its hearing is working, as suggested above?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That said, one thing I think is helping this is the complete lack of explanation of why this exists or where it came from. If anyone suggests adding it, I encourage you to refuse - this works best as an unexplained monstrosity that the Foundation happens to have around.</p>

							</div>

								
					
