		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2475257)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2475257">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/terrorforge" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(658038); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=658038&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610139848" alt="Terrorforge" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=658038)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/terrorforge" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(658038); return false;" >Terrorforge</a></span> <span class="odate time_1458337923 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2016 21:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2475257">
									

<p>I'll definitely have a look at some of those wordings. As noted the surgical steel thing is an actual classification system and the implication is that it's built out of surgical steel and stuff from radio shack, but it's not like &quot;technically correct but confusing&quot; is a good place to be.</p>
<p>As for your &quot;how do they know this&quot; comments, I think we can safely assume that the SCP document is a summary of tests and observations performed in the past and that we don't necessarily have to explain how every piece of information was acquired. I mean, how do we know that <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1193">SCP-1193</a> is &quot;genetically normal&quot;? Presumably they did a DNA test. How do we know that &quot;The nervous system of members of <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-962">SCP-962</a>-1 is slaved to implanted electrodes&quot;? Presumably they caught and dissected one at some point. How do we know that <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-860">SCP-860</a> &quot;can fit in any door lock that requires a key located in the area of the given coordinates&quot;? Presumably they tried it on every lock they could find.</p>
<p>So how do we know that SCP-2878 used to be covered in sheet steel? Well for starters it's still got bits and pieces, and presumably there's some record of what it used to look like, maybe because the damage was sustained during containment. How do we know it's smart? Presumably it demonstrated problem-solving abilities during testing or while observed in the field. How do we know that it kills people and steals their organs? Presumably they shoved a D-class into its enclosure to see what would happen. Etc., etc.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;More&nbsp;specific&nbsp;points:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;More&nbsp;specific&nbsp;points:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I find it difficult to imagine that the Foundation can ascribe psychology to something that they've only just started to communicate with. I think this would be better describing behaviour, and letting readers judge psychology for themselves.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As per above I disagree that Foundation wouldn't know anything about the way it behaves, but you have a point about show don't tell. I might try to think up something a bit more descriptive, but I'm also considering writing some testing logs to show off the weirdness.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Attacking on sight is not predatory behaviour.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I know. That's why it says &quot;and extremely hostile&quot;. In that context I feel 'predatory' is an appropriate description for something that chases down, kills, eats <em>and</em> does unspeakable things to other creatures. Do you disagree?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Addendum 1 says the relevant incident was the first time that the skip had attempted to integrate new parts. As a result, I don't think the Foundation can be so certain that this is what the skip always does to its victims.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The operative word here is &quot;new&quot;. That paragraph clearly states that the SCP normally <em>replaces</em> parts; this is the first instance of <em>adding</em> parts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And if this thing has vocal cords, but no mouth, tongue or lips, how could it possibly create sounds that are intelligible as words.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Handwaving, mostly. This thing can clearly do really weird things with both mechanical and biological parts, presumably it modified those parts in some way.</p>
<p>Also note that the interview log does imply that it <em>is</em> difficult to understand. The log makes the conversation seem clearer than it would have been at the time because it's a transcript compiled from a recording after the fact. There's actually no indication that Dr. G understands anything it's saying except for his name and the word &quot;help&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How is this thing expected to know it's name?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's policy, and therefore habit, to refer to sapient SCPs by their designation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

